"Distinguishes decision contexts so that leaders choose responses suited to ordered, complex, chaotic or unresolved conditions.\n\nUse when: Discuss why an uncertain challenge resists routine analysis. Choose between standard practice, expert investigation, probes and stabilizing action.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need a numerical risk score or a fixed classification of people.\n\nVisual grammar: Named domains describe different causal and constraint conditions. Domain boundaries have meaning and should follow the cited framework version. Short situation notes support discussion rather than precision coordinates.\n\nBuild: Describe concrete situations before assigning any domain. Discuss evidence about predictability, expertise and effective constraints. Choose an appropriate response and record conditions that would change the assessment.\n\nCheck: Complex and complicated retain distinct meanings. Domain placement describes a situation, not a permanent team identity. The diagram states which framework version its labels follow.\n\nPitfalls: Using a decorative four-quadrant matrix as a maturity ranking. Claiming retrospective explanations make a complex outcome predictable.\n\nExample: A team treats invoice checks as routine, specialist analysis as complicated and a new community program as complex, planning small learning probes.\n\nAlternatives: Risk matrix, Rich picture."
